153 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (What emerged was a view of safeguarding as a shared family responsibility, rather than something that should fall mainly on mothers. Participants felt that spending time with young people, knowing their friends and noticing changes in behaviour could help families identify problems earlier. Their comments about fathers were particularly important because they challenged the expectation that mothers should carry most of the responsibility for raising and monitoring children. For organisations working with families, this suggests that prevention work should make a deliberate effort to involve fathers, grandparents and wider family members rather than repeatedly relying on mothers to carry information and responsibility back into the home.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
